          %title
          %h3#idea Idea
          %p
            Transvection is a name for a number of concepts of involutive transformations in algebra and geometry which use shift in a direction of a fixed vector, along geodesic line (say, through another fixed point) or alike. They leave some nondegenerate hypersurface (in some contexts, hyperplane) fixed. In the geometry of symmetric spaces, one defines transvections using a parallel transport along geodesic lines; at any point these transvections induce
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+triple+system"} Lie triple system
          %blockquote
            %p
              Warning: In the usual Euclidean geometry transvection is sometimes also used as a name for
              = succeed "," do
                %em shear mapping
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shear_mapping"} shear mapping
          %h3#for_vector_spaces For vector spaces
          %p
            %strong Definition.
            For a left vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skewfield"} skewfield
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %strong transvection
            is a left
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:V\to V
            such that the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\mapsto t(v)-v
            has 1-dimensional image (called the center of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (the set of fixed points of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\mapsto t(v)-v
          %p
            For finite dimensional vector spaces over a (commutative) field,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi GL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in GL(V)
            is a transvection if it is either the identity or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo −
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim(Im(t-Id))=1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi det
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} det(t) = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi GL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\in GL(V)
            the image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo −
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Im(s-Id)
            is also called the residual space of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi res
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi dim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo −
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} res(s) = dim(Im(s-Id))
            the residue of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            iff
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mi Id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s=Id
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            are both fixed by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            and their dimensions add to the dimension of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^{-1}
            are equal to those of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          %p
            In the geometry of symplectic vector spaces (of positive dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            over a commutative field) special role is played by the transvections within the symplectic group. The fixed points of a transvection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in Sp(V)
            are precisely those points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\in V
            for which
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(v,t(v))=0
          %p
            Theorem.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(V)
            is generated by symplectic transvections (a result of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Dieudonne"} Jean Dieudonne
            for fields other than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_2
            and of D. Callan for
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_2
          %ul
            %li
              O. T. O’Meara,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on symplectic groups
              %strong 16
              (1978)
          %p
            There are similar generation results for alternating spaces (i.e. when the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is possibly degenerate, hence not symplectic) of U. Spengler and H. Wolff. For the symplectic groups over commutative rings the validity of the generation result depends on the ring, cf.
          %ul
            %li
              Yu. I. Merzlyakov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear groups
              %strong 1
              (1973) 571–593
          %p The study of transvections is often more complicated in the characteristic 2 case.
          %p
            There is a natural projection from the general linear to the projective general linear group (quotienting out by the subgroup of diagonal matrices). A
            %strong projective transvection
            is an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PGL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PGL(V)
            which is an image (under this natural projection) of a transvection in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(V)
          %h3#in_symmetric_spaces In symmetric spaces
          %p
            For transvections defined via geodesics in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+space"} symmetric space
          %ul
            %li
              J.-H. Eschenburg,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lecture notes on symmetric spaces
              %a{:href => "http://myweb.rz.uni-augsburg.de/~eschenbu/symspace.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The reflections define the Coxeter quandle what motivates the definition of transvections in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quandle"} quandle
            context. The operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi a
                  %msubsup
                    %mi R
                    %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_a R_b^{-1}
            (where
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi x
                    %mo ▹
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_a: x\mapsto x\triangleright a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
            in a quandle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            form the transvection group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Transv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Transv(X)
            which is a normal subgroup of the inner automorphism group of a quandle. For Coxeter quandle, their quotient is a cyclic group (result of D. Joyce, JPAA 1982).
          %p
            There are also quandles which are secretly entirely defined in terms of transvections; these are the transvection quandles, also called symplectic quandles; the operation is defined in terms of an alternative form on the underlying
            = succeed "-module:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %ul
            %li
              Esteban Adam Navas, Sam Nelson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On symplectic quandles
              = succeed ":4" do
                %strong 45
              %a{:href => "https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2493966"} MR2493966
              %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ojm/1227708829"} euclid
          %h3#other_literature Other literature
          %p Transvections in other contexts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J.I Hall,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Graphs, geometry, 3-transpositions, and symplectic-transvection groups
                %strong 58
                (1989) 89–111 (3)
            %li
              %p
                Hans Cuypers,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symplectic geometries, transvection groups, and modules
                = succeed ":1," do
                  %strong 65
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(94)90036-1"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Hans Cuypers. Anja Steinbach,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear transvection groups
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~kyodo/kokyuroku/contents/pdf/1063-9.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear transvection groups and embedded polar spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 137
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s002220050328"} doi
            %li
              %p
                P.J Cameron, J.I Hall,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some groups generated by transvection subgroups
                %strong 140
                (1991) 184–209
            %li
              %p
                F. Timmesfeld,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groups generated by k-transvections
                %strong 100
                (1990) 167–206
          .property
            category:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/geometry"} geometry
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/algebra"} algebra
